WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Cyrus Levinthal did the arithmetic in 1969.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.060
Even a small protein can adopt an astronomically large number of conformations.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.300
Randomly sampling each one, even at the speed of bond rotation, would take far

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.160
longer than the universe has existed.

5
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.060
The only escape from this paradox is that folding does not search randomly.

6
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.100
Instead, it follows biased pathways and stabilizes partly correct intermediates,

7
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.100
funneling rapidly downhill toward the native state.

8
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:38.083
Every constraint on protein shape traces back to the peptide bond itself.

9
00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:43.023
Because that bond has partial double bond character from resonance, the six atoms

10
00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:45.603
around it are locked flat, planarity.

11
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:50.363
That leaves only two backbone bonds free to rotate, phi and psi.

12
00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:52.403
But even those are not unrestricted.

13
00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:56.823
Steric clashes between neighboring atoms eliminate most combinations.

14
00:00:57.523 --> 00:01:03.583
The Ramachandran plot is simply the map of what survives, only about 15% of all phi

15
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:04.583
-psi space.

16
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.630
Water drives folding by pushing non-polar side chains together.

17
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.270
Burying them in a hydrophobic core releases ordered water and is entropically

18
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:18.270
favorable. Polar and charged residues sit on the surface of a soluble protein.

19
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.370
Membrane porins run the same logic in reverse.

20
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.410
Hydrophobic residues face the lipid bilayer, and a polar channel runs through the

21
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:31.530
middle. Folding is at bottom a negotiation between the protein and the chemistry of

22
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.530
water.

23
00:01:32.683 --> 00:01:37.383
Start from an astronomical number of possible conformations and four layers of

24
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:40.263
constraint progressively eliminate the wrong folds.

25
00:01:41.043 --> 00:01:44.163
Peptide bond planarity removes most options immediately.

26
00:01:44.903 --> 00:01:47.223
The preference for trans bonds cuts further.

27
00:01:47.963 --> 00:01:52.143
Ramachandran allowed phi-psi combinations constrain the backbone angles.

28
00:01:52.663 --> 00:01:57.143
Finally, the hydrophobic effect collapses the chain onto a single stable fold.

29
00:01:57.803 --> 00:01:59.203
Constraints are not limitations.

30
00:01:59.203 --> 00:02:03.343
They are the mechanism by which a sequence encodes one unique structure.

31
00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:11.087
Alpha-1 antitrypsin shows how a single amino acid change, glutamate-342 to lysine,

32
00:02:11.407 --> 00:02:14.927
causes misfolding with consequences in two separate organs.

33
00:02:15.487 --> 00:02:20.127
In the liver, the misfolded protein aggregates inside cells instead of being

34
00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:22.047
secreted, damaging the liver.

35
00:02:22.607 --> 00:02:27.847
In the lungs, the missing circulating protein leaves the enzyme elastase unchecked,

36
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:31.087
slowly destroying lung tissue and causing emphysema.

37
00:02:31.687 --> 00:02:37.487
One mutation, two organ systems: a vivid demonstration of why folding fidelity

38
00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:37.927
matters.
